in Chapter two has finally hit the big  screen and while the epic conclusion to  the adaptation of Stephen King's 1986  novel  it has largely left fans satisfied a  fair number of critics weren't so  impressed fans of the original book in  the films are likely to shoot down a few  of the pervasive criticisms of the flick  with these arguments several changes  were made to the character of Derry  library resident Mike Hanlon and  criticisms around these changes can be  confusing all of the losers are bullied  but in King's novel Henry Bowers  bullying of Mike was particularly awful  because of Bauer's unrestrained racism  some observers took issue with the fact  that in both movies the racist element  of the bullying against Mike was  substantially toned down leading to  charges of squeamishness on the part of  director Andy muschietti and  screenwriter Gary Doberman  but the racist nature of the Bowers  gangs bullying of Mike is certainly  implied if no racial slurs are used on  screen  [Music]  and it's worth remembering that the  films are set 31 years later than the  novel with chapter 1 set in 1989 rather  than 1958 racism is of course still  pervasive today but it's not a huge  stretch to say that the use of evert  slurs was more likely to be encountered  in the 50s than the 80s and neither film  really suffered from the filmmakers  decision to make the racial aspect of  Bauer's antagonism toward Mike more  subtle the decision was the obvious  byproduct not only of the time period  switch but of adapting a 33 year old  novel  another difference between literary Mike  and cinematic Mike is the fate of his  parents they were incorporated into  Mike's tragic backstory in movies having  burned to death inside a locked  apartment while young Mike watched  helplessly a newspaper headline  conspicuously shown in it chapter 2  refers to a pair of crackheads who set  themselves on fire in front of their  young son but this is later revealed to  be one of penny wisest tricks after the  malevolent entity is defeated the true  headline is revealed and it's made clear  that Mike's parents actually died in an  electrical fire the reason for this  change is fairly obvious for narrative  reasons the poor kid needed a good dose  of trauma into the fake headline place  off of the Bowers gangs racially charged  bullying but one spectacularly wrong  headed review by slates Jack Hamilton  opined that the change itself smacked of  racism he wrote to transform the only  black protagonist from the child of  responsible nurturing parents into the  child of negligent crack cocaine addicts  is far worse than lazy writing it's to  actively draw from a deeply racist set  of cultural tropes we guess he must have  been yawning really wide or looking at  his watch when the true newspaper  headline was revealed of all the  critical gripes levelled at it Chapter  two by far the most common had to do  with the film's nearly three hour  runtime  the film was labeled arduous muddled and  meandering why  for you of course Kings novel is more  than 1100 pages long and a huge amount  of material was left out in bringing the  story to the screen some elements were  combined into details in the film's  story such as the fire that killed  Mike's parents being a clear reference  to the novel's fire at the black spot a  historical event which was used to  illustrate penny wisest centuries-long  influence on Derry those going into it  chapter 2 expecting a brisk breeze aliy  plotted ride to the final showdown  either weren't familiar with the source  material or were expecting Doberman and  muschietti to take far more liberties  than they did the individual and  collective journeys of the losers Club  played directly into how they deal with  the horror of that final confrontation  and if they had been given short shrift  it's likely that many of these same  critics would have been complaining  about that many of it chapter 2 'the  critics also took issue with the  tumorous tone in particular the  wisecracks the adult members of the  losers Club are prone to lobbing at each  other as they prepare to face off  against a heart so unimaginable that  they almost all forgot their first  encounter with it much of the humor is  even intertwined with the horror as with  a brilliant visual homage to John  Carpenter's 1982 classic the thing but  it's clear for some of the characters  the humour is there to mask the fear  it's the old whistling past the  graveyard trope one last so that they  may not soil themselves out of sheer  terror the losers Club is an irreverent  Bunch in both the novel and the film's  in the banter doesn't just have the  effect of entertaining the audience it  also keeps them from going insane if  anything the humor is even dialed down  from the book perhaps the most  frustrating reviews of it chapter 2 were  those that completely whiffed in  pinpointing the film's themes take for  example this excerpt from the review of  NBC News's Noah Berlitz key chapter 2  presents itself as an exploration of  nostalgia and memory the adults  magically forget their childhood  adventures when they move away from  Derry and the movie is about them  reacquainting themselves with their past  friendships and with their true selves  upon their return what the losers are  reacquainting themselves with  voluntarily or not is the horror of  their youth the searing inescapable  trauma which many of us deal with and  which is often buried not by some magic  but as a form of emotional self-defense  doberman spelled this out in an  interview with inverse  think as an adult we all have some  repressed trauma somewhere in us I'm the  same age as the losers in it chapter 2 I  followed up on my own childhood and how  easy it is for memories to fade even the  more traumatic ones like getting details  wrong or asking did that happen when I  can't remember the esoteric ending of  kings novel is practically unfilmable  the filmmakers behind it chapter 2  offered up their own interpretation and  of course many critics jumped all over  it as a monstrous Pennywise menaces them  the losers essentially take away his  power by ridiculing him until he's  physically small enough that they can  rip out and destroy his still-beating  heart at least one observer came  somewhat close to the mark by stating  that the losers sort of bully Pennywise  to death during a conversation with  muschietti only for the director to set  him straight I didn't see it as bullying  so much as it was about reversing the  power of belief on this creature who  uses belief as a weapon to kill his  victims for the first time it's the  losers who have this power and they  start telling him what he is  you're a headless boy you're a weak old  woman and finally they converge on the  concept of a clown which is just the  most helpless and powerless thing the  power of belief is penny wisest power  but it's also his biggest weakness so it  is with all bullies whose victims must  feel weak for them to feel powerful  penny wise is simply the biggest bully  of them all so once that power of belief  is taken away he has nothing left we  just stick together check out one of her  newest videos right here plus even more  looper videos but your favorite movies  are coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the belt so you don't  miss a single one  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

